         <p class="eyebrow">Module SC1 · Tier M · 100 hours · Part SC, Stochastic Calculus &amp; Continuous Time</p>
         <h1>Stochastic Processes</h1>
-        <p class="lede">Many models of prices and noise describe a quantity that moves randomly through time, and the questions that matter about such a process are about its paths: how far it can move in a short time, how its sums of squared moves behave, when it first reaches a level, and how its running maximum is distributed. This module develops the continuous-time theory those questions need, starting from Brownian motion. It defines the process by its start and its increments, constructs it with continuous paths, computes its quadratic variation, and develops the martingale, Markov and stopping-time properties. It ends with the reflection principle, which gives the distribution of the running maximum and of the first-passage time in closed form under Assumption A3. Its project proves those results and checks them with a module that runs on generated instances.</p>
+        <p class="lede">Many models of prices and noise describe a quantity that moves randomly through time, and the questions that matter about such a process are about its paths: how far it can move in a short time, how its sums of squared moves behave, when it first reaches a level, and how its running maximum is distributed. This module develops the continuous-time theory those questions need, starting from Brownian motion. It defines the process by its start and its increments, constructs it with continuous paths, computes its quadratic variation, and develops the martingale, Markov and stopping-time properties. It ends with the reflection principle, which gives the distribution of the running maximum and of the first-passage time in closed form under Assumption A3 (stated in Lesson 5). Its project proves those results and checks them with a module that runs on generated instances.</p>
